A||||49||Shaun41178||Turbo Bandwagon||||1||
Z||000000||Shaun41178||08-31-2001||11:58 PM||||My goodness.  WTF is going on with everyone jumping on the turbo bandwagon lately?  I don't get it.  From what I have read many of you don't even know how a turbo works or what has to be done to install one, yet all of you want someone to do it for you for a 6 pack of beer and a cheeseburger.  You havent' said that but I know most of you are thinking "kit" for under $1k which is a crock <P>Design One Systems has already taken care of the work for you.  Go buy one of their kits.  No guess work.  You want someone else to do it for you but do you really think that someone can do it for much cheaper?  Not by very much unless all used parts are used in the install.  But why would you want to have used parts?  Hardest part is going to be the computer controls and how it handles boost.  DES 1 has made a custom prom already for their kit.  Someone making a one off kit for someone else going to supply a custom chip too?  My guess would be no cause that is where a lot of the cost of adding a turbo comes in.  If thats not programmed correctly then BOOOOM!  Which has happened to one person already on this forum.  And if you don't know how a turbo works then how are you going to know what else has to be done to get it to run right?<P><BR>And second why is everyone on the T-3 tip?  In my opinion the T-3 is too small for a 2.8, 3.2, 3.4.  It was put on a 2.3 liter engine originally.  So you putting it on an engine from half a liter to a full liter bigger is insame.  The turbo is just not big enough and creates a lot of heat.  The more heat the less boost you can safely run without an intercooler.  We all know the Fiero engine is grunty down low so it doesn't need help there.  So don't use a small turbo to add low end torque.  It has enough of that already.  Get a turbo for more top end since that is where the engine lacks power.  Get a t-60 or a t3-t4 hybrid.  Or get a 20G for a Talon.  I have seen t-60's used on 4 cylinders and they flat out haul top end.<P>Also with a larger turbo you can run the same amount of boost as with a smaller turbo but make more power.  Thats cause a larger turbo will flow more air at less boost then a smaller turbo with more boost.  So basically you can make the same power on 5 psi with a larger turbo then with 8 psi with a smaller turbo.  Less boost equals less heat which makes it easier on the pistons.<P>And don't forget that once you get this all 9installed you will need to have it dyno tuned.  At $100/hour it will take a good 2 hours or more to get it right and within the proper air/fuel ratios.  Hey speed costs money.  It isn't cheap.  You want cheap get nitrous.  For $500 you can have about 170 hp at the wheels and turn low 14's in the qtr like me.<P>I suggest that most of you go out and pick up a book and start reading.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierosound||09-01-2001||12:36 AM||||Well put.  I've noticed a similar trend where "best " and "cheapest" pop up in the same sentence, often dealing with engine swaps, headers, etc.  There's always a "good, better, best"  scenario with prices matched accordingly.  Then there's always the "good enough" route, which may be most people's best alternative once they discover it...||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||09-01-2001||12:39 AM||||I agree; that's why I'm going nitrous this winter, if I get to keep my car.  I know how that stuff works.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroMaster88||09-01-2001||12:48 AM||||Well...those of us that know something about turbo's or have read up on how they work know what is required to run them and are accually serious about doing it!  I plan on building my engine AROUND the turbo.....have everything worked out and ready for the turbo and the boost.  And I dont think it is gonna be "cheap" or "easy".....there is a lot of initial cost with a turbo...and a lot of prep work for it.  But you dont have to refil a turbo like NOS.  Plus, a turbo provides boost all the time, where NOS only does it when you press the button.  So sure...nitrous is cheaper and easier and more cost effective to begin with, but the turbo pays for itself over the long haul.<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Shaun41178:<BR><B><BR>You want cheap get nitrous.  For $500 you can have about 170 hp at the wheels and turn low 14's in the qtr like me.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">            <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe With a new V6<BR>5 speed Isuzu trans<BR>AcCel 8mm wires<BR>rapid fire plugs<BR>custom intake with K&N filter.<BR>Soon to be 2" closer to the ground and have a turbo||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||09-01-2001||02:00 AM||||I've built my motor around the fact that I want to have turbo'd eventually(tough components and low compression.)  I won't do a design1 kit simply because of cost, and because I'm not a fan of the turbo that it includes.  Besides that, I want this to be my own creation.  I'll admit that when I first decided that I wanted a turbo, I didn't even understand how they worked.  The first thing I did was buy an in-depth book and READ READ READ.  I encourage everybody to read a GOOD turbocharging book before even attempting a turbo setup.  I'm having the plumbing fabricated by a local import shop called Excessive Autosports so that's it done right and professional.  Shaun, I'm with you on the turbo size... I know that T3 setups have been done successfully, but it just doesn't seem like enough.  Especially because of the fact that I'm running a 4.10 tranny.  The last thing I want is to be boosting strong at highway cruizing speed and cook my motor because I didn't want to pay for a bigger turbo. After careful consideration, I felt that the turbo was the only way that I'd be truely happy with the performance, but DO THE RESEARCH for god-sake.  Utilize a BOV, buy the MSD-BTM, upgrade your fuel system, keep the boost low to start with(below 7lbs), get an air/fuel meter so that you KNOW how the fuel mixture is, and make sure you're running a boost gauge.  And last but not least, make sure that you make it a point to fish-tail around corners, do massive burnouts, and race people through residential neighborhoods.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/gtbastard.gif">     <P>"My heroes are John Wayne, the Simpsons, and whoever canceled Full House/Bob Saget."||||reg||1
Z||000005||00lE||09-01-2001||06:32 AM||||Kinda strange that GM would use such a small turbo on the 89-90 3.1 Turbo Grand Prix, basically a T-28.  Perhaps the engineers just didn't know what they were doing, or maybe they just know what turbo lag is. The TGP even has a pretty decent engine managment system, and it would probably work for other GM 60° V6 turbo applications as well..wow!  <P>I think that most of these guys are just looking for a way to obtain a bit more power out of an engine in a 10-12yr old car with average miles and a stock compression ratio.  Bolting an extremely large turbocharger onto a tired stock engine such as this may not be a real great idea.      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> There is also very little space for an adequate intercooler, so boost levels over 8-10lbs are pretty much out of the question anyway.<BR>Unless you work to overcome these limitations, including the small amount of space available in the Fiero engine compartment, a smaller turbo such as the T3 would be a logical choice.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 09-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||09-01-2001||07:53 AM||||thats why I keep recommending a kit. I have the Design One kit and never had a problem from day 1. I got it used but uninstalled for 1/2 of list on Ebay. There must have been 50 parts in the kit - not just a junkyard turbo welded on the exhaust pipe.||||reg||1
Z||000007||turbo86se||09-01-2001||11:08 AM||||Personally, I think it's great there are so many people that want to create their own turbo system for their Fieros. I'm with Roger, it may cost you a little more, but a kit is the best way. You get everything you need, and a warranty to fall back on. My turbo was installed on my Fiero WAY before I owned it. Barbra Sutter of IRM still remembers the car and after many coversations with Jim Sutter, help and replacement parts are a phone call away. Of course the satisfaction of fabricating your own kit is unmatched.<P>An air/fuel guage sounds interesting. Are they hard to install? I saw some cool fuel guages at the Williamsburg show I'd like to install, where could I buy such a guage. The only guage my MillerWoods turbo system has is a boost guage. <P>It's always fun when people come and go, talk about turboing their Fieros...<BR>I just walk out to the garage and drool over mine!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Predator||09-01-2001||12:57 PM||||I have a Design One Systems Stage II kit. I am not even going to go there with the ECM. It seems not to be programmed that great from my experiance. I am still having to have a Rising rate fuel presure regulator installed to compensate under boost. If you go with a Turbo you should have it Dyno tested to make absolutley sure you are not going lean under boost. A A/F guage is not going to give you accurate enough information, and when you are screaming down the road you probably should not be staring at a guage the whole time either. If you do a home grown Turbo then I would definitly have a professional check it out before even driving it. If you are wrong on your fuel ratio setup you won't get a second chance to correct it. A turbo setup done right is not cheap. Goto <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/009955.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/009955.html</A>  to see a Dyno run with a Design One Systems Stage II turbo kit. With the FPR I hope to break 200 rear wheel hp.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by Predator (edited 09-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||00lE||09-01-2001||01:10 PM||||     You really don't need a dyno to tune a car for a turbo.  A decent hand held scanner should tell you everything you need to know.<BR>     If you are good with a soldering iron, you could throw an interface cable together for pocket change, and use a laptop to obtain even better capability (ie. data logging) then a scanner.<BR>     I agree that some people should just buy a kit and have it installed. But, there are many talented people on here that could do a lot better than the design one kit, and for quite a bit less the 3 large.<p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 09-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Predator||09-01-2001||02:25 PM||||Not trying to start something here. Everyone is entitled to their opinion, but if you can make a complete Turbo kit, Fuel management system, exhaust mods, plumbing, Turbo, ECM changes, be completely emissions and street legal, and so forth that someone can just bolt onto their car for under $3,000 then I suggest you build it and market it. You could make a killing. If you can tune the A/F under boost without putting the car under load then that’s great. I did not know that. You can save a Dyno run then. I would never say there are not people who could do it, but I seriously doubt there are many who can do the setup from ground zero right for under $3,000.||||reg||1
Z||000011||sluppy123||09-01-2001||03:03 PM||||There has been a ton of talk of turbos on the Forum lately, Maybe some of it is because of me? I think I'm the only one on the forum that has actually Installed a turbo from scratch, other that west coast fiero. There are a ton of people who are planning a install, and judging by the # of email's I've gotten, not very many of them really know how to do it. <BR>As for Shaun41178 comment about NOS, Yes it's cheap, easy to install, very good bang for the buck. I know this, I had a 70hp shot of NOS on my car before I went with the turbo. But NOS isn't there all the time, that's what I didn't like it.<BR>One of the reasons I didn't go out and buy a turbo KIT is I wanted to say "I built it". <BR>If you know How to do something, Have the right equipment to do it with, Why not just do it yourself? and to all the people that say it can't be done, O well<BR><P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>87' GT-Galaxy silver mettalic, Turbo Charged, Modified 2.8- 235 HP w/ 7 psi Boost, Eibach Sport springs, RSM side scoops, Mr Mikes leather, 17" Mille Miglia's. <BR>               <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1711832&a=12781563&p=48213929&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||artherd||09-01-2001||07:59 PM||||Hi Shaun, I too am sick of all the cheapskates that inhabit Fiero-land.  I'm not talking about those of us who are *creative* and find clever ways of getting the most from our hard-earned $, but the kind of people who put on used brake lines for example (that's just dangerously cheapassed.)<P>I suppose it is to be expected when you have what is really a high performance, *exotic* car, with almost no resale/purchase value.  <P>I agree with most everything you've stated above, but have to question one thing:<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Shaun41178:<BR><B><BR>Also with a larger turbo you can run the same amount of boost as with a smaller turbo but make more power.  Thats cause a larger turbo will flow more air at less boost then a smaller turbo with more boost.  So basically you can make the same power on 5 psi with a larger turbo then with 8 psi with a smaller turbo.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You're correct about the temprature issues, but you also say that higher 'flow' helps at a given PSI...     Now, automotive internal combustion engines are fixed, posative-displacement devices, so they consume air at a rate directly proportional to their RPMs, no more, no less (at a given manifold pressure here remenber.)<P>What I am saying is that any intake charge of 5PSI at 100degrees F (let's just randomly say) should be the same, *regardless* of the turbo, supercharger, or tank of regulated hot compressed air, that produced it.  The engine *does not care* and flow (so long as it is sufficent to maintain 5psi/100f throught the entire revband of the engine's demand) will be exctly the same at any given RPM.<P>Now, if we start talking about a turbo that is too small to flow full boost pressure at the engine's redline, that's a whole different story.<P>Sorry for the long-windedness, if I were an elloquent writer, I could probally have condensed that to two lines  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000013||terryk||09-01-2001||10:10 PM||||After having done a couple of Fiero swaps, the turbo is starting to have some merit. I've got two new one's I've been measuring out for my other 86SE. A bit of flips and tweaks on the chip and it should be run fine. I'm not sure I'd go with the gazillion pounds of boost, but &lt; 10PSI should work.<P>I have a 3.2L with 8.5:1 compression so that gives me some breathing room.<P>Sluppy, how did you handle the expansion and contraction of the crossover pipe? <P>The manual trans makes it tighter too.<P>Also, is the turbo supported by the crossover or did you make a mount to the trans?<P><BR>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 09-01-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 09-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||sluppy123||09-01-2001||10:25 PM||||I made a spring loaded mount to hold the turbo up, it's bolted to the top of the bell housing. All the weight of the turbo is supported by the mount, which can move with the crossover and downpipe as they expand and contract.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mach10||09-02-2001||02:13 PM||||Explain to me how a larger turbo will move more air into a cylinder with LESS pressure?!? Doesn't matter what size turbo you're using, you're still ramming X amount of air through a 52mm (stock, right?) bore throttlebody... How are you going to do that without more pressure?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> Maybe I'm missing something...<P>Regardless, I don't see why anyone HAS to go with a prefab kit. If you skimp on the important details, you'll litter the road with parts. Same with Nitrous. <BR>I'm planning on doing my own "junkyard" setup. Do you have a problem with this? I'm doing my homework. I'm having the turbo rebuilt, and a spare motor to build up. If I rip my engine to shreds within the first 10 runs after breakin, I will personally mail you a Coke. True, the Junkyard is a cost-saving measure. Not because I'm going lowest-bidder, but so that I'll have more money to dump into the valvetrain and bottom end. If my turbo frags, oh well. I'll pick the shards of hot metal out of my skull and get another.<P>Don't assume that everyone that's going the cheaper route isn't doing it PROPERLY.<P>*gets on the bandwagon*||||reg||5
Z||000016||Predator||09-02-2001||03:37 PM||||[Regardless, I don't see why anyone HAS to go with a prefab kit. If you skimp on the important details, you'll litter the road with parts. Same with Nitrous.]<P>Most people in general don’t have the time, equipment, or skill level to create a turbo setup no more than everybody here could fabricate the setup needed for a V8 swap. If everybody could then people like V8Archie would be out of business. I think you are taking the topic of this post to seriously. There are a lot of people here who want a lot and want to pay nothing for it, and I think that is all this post was pointing out.  <P>[Do you have a problem with this?]<P>Hmm no should I?<P>[I rip my engine to shreds within the first 10 runs after breakin, I will personally mail you a Coke.]<P>Is that for everybody on the forum?<P>[If my turbo frags, oh well. I'll pick the shards of hot metal out of my skull and get another.]<P>Remind me not to be around when you do this. Picking shards of hot metal out of my head does not sound like fun to me anyway. <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Slammed Fiero||09-02-2001||04:35 PM||||Shaun Brings up a lot of Valid points. I'm waiting for him to comment on the kid who is going to build a 3.4ltr with 400hp , and he knows a guy at MClaren and is going to get Mclaren suspension , and make his own crank<BR>and yadda yadda yaddaa<P>A lot of these projects and cars you hear about on the net aren't 1/10th as good as thee owners would have you believe.<P>Lots of knowledgeable people out there though. Just have to sift through the BS.<P>JM <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||09-02-2001||06:57 PM||||Experience is what toned down my "target" horsepower. I'd either pick some abritary big number or read what some people claim was possible and then say "I'm targetting 400HP". <P>Eventually, you learn what is realistic and the numbers come way down. They come down even more after you drive over the crank once.<P>I've got the stuff to do it, I just need to decide it's what I want to do. Project #659.||||reg||1
Z||000019||artherd||09-04-2001||01:49 AM||||I think the 'problem' is with people who then ask "ok, so which end of this whirly-gig do I plug into my engine anyway?"  <P>Heh.<P>I went with a used (20k) engine on my Northstar project so I could spend the money elsewhere (engine computer, twin-disc clutch, etc.)<P>Good luck with your project, it will be quite a learning expierence for sure, and you stand a good chance of getting a reallly fast fiero out of it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B><P>Don't assume that everyone that's going the cheaper route isn't doing it PROPERLY.<P>*gets on the bandwagon*</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000020||Smoooooth GT||09-04-2001||10:09 AM||||I went with the *cheaper version* for now...  nos kit...  I spent a little over 300 and still have nothing!!!  If you go this cheap route, DON'T BUY USED....  MINE DOES'NT WORK???  I can't figure why either....  Just my .02 cent's...  Steve<P>------------------<BR>NOS Injected 2.8L<BR>Future add's.. 2" drop, 17" wheel's, finish the turbo T-3watercooled project, ViperYellow <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooth_sig2_4.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||1FST2M6||09-04-2001||01:05 PM||||kinda looks like Shauns just tired of so many talkin and so few actually doing.  <P><BR>when the rain finally stops around here i'll be making mpegs of a few smokey 18s and a Donut or two... any takers? <P>already on the e-mailing list is Predator, GT, and Shaun... <P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstmeatsig.jpg">          <BR>www.engineered.net||||reg||1
Z||000022||Predator||09-04-2001||01:12 PM||||I look forward to seeing them man. I can't wait to see what the car is like after Ed puts the FPR on, and Dyno's it again. It would be really cool to break 200 RWHP. That would pretty much do it for me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000023||FieroMaster88||09-04-2001||01:12 PM||||Hell yeah.  I want to see that!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Send me a MPEG!  Just dont get too dizzy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">            <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe With a new V6<BR>5 speed Isuzu trans<BR>AcCel 8mm wires<BR>rapid fire plugs<BR>custom intake with K&N filter.<BR>Soon to be 2" closer to the ground and have a turbo||||reg||14
Z||000024||Mach10||09-04-2001||01:20 PM||||Who is Predator? Oh well...\<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Most people in general don’t have the time, equipment, or skill level to create a turbo setup no more than everybody here could fabricate the setup needed for a V8 swap. If everybody could then people like V8Archie would be out of business. I think you are taking the topic of this post to seriously. There are a lot of people here who want a lot and want to pay nothing for it, and I think that is all this post was pointing out.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Agreed. But I don't think that skill level is much of a factor. It's a learning experience. Now, if you don't have the necessary skills, then it is in your best interests to consult someone who does. Part of the whole deal is to pick reasonable expectations. The 400hp 3.4, for example. Yes, it CAN be done. Not cheap. Plus, it will most likely self-destruct after a few thousand miles. With power, you sacrifice long-term reliability, and economy.<BR>Personally, I'm shooting for 6-8lbs of boost, with around 200-220HP out of a built 2.8. My readings have told me that this is not unreasonable. I WILL be using a smaller turbo system. The T-3 with a 60 trim should be fine for this application. The BOV goes without saying, as does a custom PROM. It won't be cheap, but using the rebuilt Junkyard turbo will help me buy all the important ancilliaries. <P>Oh, and I do disagree. Picking hot shards of twisted metal out of your skull is the most fun you can have on a saturday afterneen with a case of beer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>1FST2M6: Sign me up...||||reg||1
Z||000025||Standard||09-04-2001||01:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR><B>kinda looks like Shauns just tired of so many talkin and so few actually doing.  <P><BR>when the rain finally stops around here i'll be making mpegs of a few smokey 18s and a Donut or two... any takers? <P>already on the e-mailing list is Predator, GT, and Shaun... <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>put me on that list  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P> <A HREF="mailto:lastminutebastrd@aol.com">lastminutebastrd@aol.com</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000026||Predator||09-04-2001||04:02 PM||||Sounds like a plan Mach10  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<BR>If it helps I am getting 178 RWHP from my turbo setup with minor mods to the engine. ||||reg||6
Z||000027||Shaun41178||09-04-2001||04:51 PM||||Steve the kit worked when I sent it to you.  I tested it before I took it apart to make certain of it.<P>Something must be wired wrong or yes maybe a solenoid did go bad.  I doubt that though but it is possible.  The one solenoid with the filter connected in it is practically brand new so I doubt it is that one.  Check all the grounds with a test light and test to make sure that power is getting to the solenoids.  Are you sure the WOT switch is going to ground?  how much does the bottle weigh?<P>Please call me so I can talk you through this and try and help you out.  Through the computer thing here is just too hard.<P>904-615-9916  Believe me we will get it working.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Voytek||09-04-2001||06:29 PM||||Speaking of turbos... Does anyone know of the best and cheap turbo setup for my fiero?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000029||Pontiaddict||09-04-2001||06:31 PM||||1FST2M6<BR>Please add me to that list.<BR>mehere@zxmail.com||||reg||1
Z||000030||Mach10||09-04-2001||07:25 PM||||Yeah, I'd <B>LOVE</B> 200Hp at the wheels, but I will settle for 200 at the crank. I do plan on using a small turbo at low boost for the simple reason of NO lag. 'Specially since the 2.8's got some grunt. This thing will be FAST off the line. <BR> <BR>The hardest part of the rebuild will be to coax 6500rpm out of that pushrod motor, and to keep the damn thing together. I'm going to need an 88 or later block with the better oiling for sure. The second hardest part will be picking the right cam. Custom grinds may be the way to go, but expensive  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>To tell the truth, I have no idea how I'm going to handle the ECM, though. What I'd REALLY like is to have one with provisions for a knock-sensor. Don't know who I'd get to program that, though... I'll cross that bridge when I get to it.||||reg||6
Z||000031||tomcat06085||09-04-2001||07:34 PM||||178 HP sounds good to me...i would rather have usable horses than broken horses<P>||||reg||14
Z||000032||Cooter||09-04-2001||07:52 PM||||Mach10- get a GMC Syclone ECM and prom. It will work with the Fiero with minimal fueling changes. There are folks around that can tune the chip for you, or bite the bullet and get the stuff to program your own. You will not be disappointed! I have been running one for months and performance is great. Now, if I only had time to actually get the blower installed!||||reg||1
Z||000033||DreXteR||09-04-2001||10:55 PM||||Cooter - Let me know if I understand you. I can use a Syclone ECM and Prom to the fiero harness and work!?<P>If so, The prom alone? <P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000034||Smoooooth GT||09-05-2001||01:42 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Shaun41178:<BR><B>Steve the kit worked when I sent it to you.  I tested it before I took it apart to make certain of it.<P>Something must be wired wrong or yes maybe a solenoid did go bad.  I doubt that though but it is possible.  The one solenoid with the filter connected in it is practically brand new so I doubt it is that one.  Check all the grounds with a test light and test to make sure that power is getting to the solenoids.  Are you sure the WOT switch is going to ground?  how much does the bottle weigh?<P>Please call me so I can talk you through this and try and help you out.  Through the computer thing here is just too hard.<P>904-615-9916  Believe me we will get it working.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Cool...  I did'nt mean anything by my comment..  it's probably nothing. I'll call if I can't get it to work. Thank's Steve<P>------------------<BR>NOS Injected 2.8L<BR>Future add's.. 2" drop, 17" wheel's, finish the turbo T-3watercooled project, ViperYellow <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooth_sig2_4.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000035||Cooter||09-05-2001||07:25 PM||||DreX- you will need the complete ECM and wiring plugs for the new ECM. The '730 ecm (F body with TPI) uses the same plugs and are easy to find in the junkyard. Get the ECM pinout diagrams for the Syclone and Fiero. Start with the first wire in the Fiero plug, remove it and snap it into the correct place in the Syclone ECM. After a couple of hours, you will be ready to roll. The wires are NOT in the same places between ECM's. There are a few issues to deal with- a manual shift PROM for a syclone is pretty hard to come by, but the automatic one I have works pretty good in my car. The speedometer wiring gets a little funky, but it can be made to work fine. And, No- a Syclone prom will not fit a Fiero ECM.||||reg||1
Z||000036||Mach10||09-05-2001||11:14 PM||||So the Syclone ECM/PROM combo has provisions for a knock sensor? I'd actually like to bastardize a ECM/PROM from a factory turbo car, so that I can have the built-in failsafes like over-boost and wastegate bypass. Then I'd have some more armor between me and a sorry looking pile of expensive, melted parts.||||reg||7
Z||000037||Cooter||09-05-2001||11:35 PM||||The Syclone setup allows for a knock sensor, automatic pump activation of your air/water intercooler pump, programable wastegate valve, timing retard/fuel enrichment under boost, and a lot of other stuff that I really don't understand. Even things like the A/C compressor turn on delay after starting can be adjusted in the PROM. I did not have to change any of the underhood wiring (except to modify the MAP sensor plug to fit a 2 bar MAP). A couple of new wires were added- one for the knock sensor (my 3.4 already had one in it) and another one that I can't remember. The wiring colors for the 2 different ECM's are the same, just take extra care in where they go into the new ECM. If you want to go aluminum head and DIS, a '727 ecm and PROM from a turbo Grand Prix ('89 or '90 I think) will work for that. I have one of those ECM's and PROMS around here already if you are going the aluminum head route. I had my car running with the '727 and DIS brick, but it was not right for the iron head motor for some reason.||||reg||1
Z||000038||DreXteR||09-06-2001||12:50 AM||||Cooter - Lets say, my car is a 2.8 iron everything and manual tranny. Wich I should look for? How much are they? <P>Thanx <P>DreX<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000039||00lE||09-06-2001||02:09 PM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I had my car running with the '727 and DIS brick, but it was not right for the iron head motor for some reason.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If the difference in compression ratio resulting from head used was addressed properly, it should have no relative effect on the PCM's operation.<P>The 1227727 w/TGP code is the best choice for the turbocharged 3.4, or turbo applications that use the newer 3.1 block, since it provides DIS capability.  For a setup that must use a distributor, the Sy/Ty ecm & code may work, but I suspect there would be some major issues with fuel and volumetric efficency related tables.  One should pay particular attention to injector pulse width at WOT when doing a pcm switch such as this, since the larger injector constant used in the SyTy PCM code would logically result in a very lean burn during open loop mode (eg. warmup or WOT) on a smaller engine.<BR>IOW, indicating that this would be anything near a plug-n-play swap would be VERY bad advise, IMHO.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 09-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000040||Mach10||09-06-2001||05:33 PM||||I knew that I'd have to have the PROM fuel maps redone, I didn't consider the Syclone to be a drop in. It'd be nice to know someone who knows ECMs inside out so I could build a better fuel table. Failing that, a few runs on a dyno with an 02 meter and a screw-driver on the adjustable fuel pressure regulator would have to suffice. I'd rather run too rich than too lean with a turbo  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000041||Cooter||09-06-2001||10:38 PM||||As 00lE mentioned, it has not been a drop in replacement. After I replaced the old fuel pump and filter, it 14 new tries at tuning the chip to get it where I was satisfied with the performance. I still have not mounted the blower, so I can't tell you what happens under boost. Hopefully, the kind gentleman that I bought my tuner software from will still offer some advice. What I recommend doing is find someone in your area that has the ability to do the tuning before you attempt such a project. I have been lucky, but my luck may not apply to you   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Cooter (edited 09-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000042||Mach10||09-07-2001||02:02 AM||||Heh... You can do me a favor and package up the ROM image up for me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Take some of the guesswork out of it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000043||Cooter||09-07-2001||08:24 AM||||I can't do that because my car and your car are way too different. And I'd hate to lose a friend when my chip toasts your engine. It seems like 00lE really knows his stuff. Maybe we should start a new thread on ECM tuning so the resident experts can give some advice on this, after all this is something we could all eventually benefit from.||||reg||1
Z||000044||BV MotorSports||09-07-2001||01:54 PM||||"Get a turbo for more top end since that is where the engine lacks power. Get a t-60 or a t3-t4 hybrid. Or get a 20G for a Talon. I have seen t-60's used on 4 cylinders and they flat out haul top end."<P>A T3/T4 is not a good choice for a street driven fiero. The turbo lag would be horrible and if you competed with this car in autoX or road racing it would be way difficult to power out of the corners because the boost would come on to hard. T3/T4 wouldnt even start producing decent boost till about 3500rpm on a fiero. So you end up with a big money turbo that is usefull for 2500rpm. No thanks. Who drives at 6,000 rpm all the time this side of a honda? Get a turbo that is sized for how you drive and you will be much happier. I own several turbocharged cars. 1 even has a t3/t4 and its a bit big for street use. To much lag. I like the Mitsu 18g or 17c much better.<P>A Mitsu 12A, 14g or (for modded fieros)16g would be damn near perfect for a turbo charged Fiero. The 12A is rated @ 365 CFM @ 10psi. A 14G is rated @ 495 cfm.<BR>Both are both oil and water cooled. A 12A can be rebuilt and upgraded to 14g for under $200.<BR>I personally wouldnt turbocharge any car w/o using an intercooler but looking at the fiero engine bay, I can see why most dont take advantage of intercooling. <BR>I think what Dennis L. pioneered for do it yourself guys is awsome. We need more people with that kinda of additude. <BR>Boy, I cant wait to get my turbo fiero done.<P>Steven<BR><P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 89mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>87 Conquest TSi 406 SBC V8!<BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||1
Z||000045||00lE||09-07-2001||11:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BV MotorSports:<BR><B>"A Mitsu 12A, 14g or (for modded fieros)16g would be damn near perfect for a turbo charged Fiero. The 12A is rated @ 365 CFM @ 10psi. A 14G is rated @ 495 cfm.<BR>Both are both oil and water cooled. A 12A can be rebuilt and upgraded to 14g for under $200.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree. There are many turbo's available for mitsu's that would work very well on a pushrod V6.  Most are intended for the 2.0 4cyl, but these engine's operate with a much higher redline and better overall volumetric efficiency then the typical pr 2.8-3.4, so in most cases they are a fairly good match.    The 2nd gen eclipse guys all seem to enjoy swapping their near new T-25's for 1st gen TD05's, and they usually sell these low mileage T-25's for near salvage prices(&lt; $200).  Twin T-25's would make for one damn fast turbocharged 3.4L Fiero with nearly insignificant lag! :-)<BR>I believe I posted something a while back about my solution to the Fiero intercooler dilema.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000046||00lE||09-08-2001||12:00 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cooter:<BR><B>Maybe we should start a new thread on ECM tuning so the resident experts can give some advice on this, after all this is something we could all eventually benefit from.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's a good idea.  I do not consider myself an expert, but there are a few of those around. :-)  I do have all the prom burning equipment and I even manage to produce some fairly good results once in a while.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 09-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000047||Mach10||09-08-2001||01:22 PM||||Sounds good to me. Personally, I was planning on a T-25 at around 6-8lbs. Quick spoolup, decent power, not THAT hard on the engine. As for ECM stuff, I'd be quite happy to slap in a Syclone ECM/PROM, but I would need help with the fuel maps. I'd love to get a burner and be able to customize my own. Maybe a couple of years of CompSci is enough background to start learning this crap? I don't know where I'd start. Any ideas?<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000048||Mach10||09-08-2001||04:52 PM||||Bump?||||reg||1
Z||000049||00lE||09-09-2001||12:16 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Sounds good to me. Personally, I was planning on a T-25 at around 6-8lbs. Quick spoolup, decent power, not THAT hard on the engine. As for ECM stuff, I'd be quite happy to slap in a Syclone ECM/PROM, but I would need help with the fuel maps. I'd love to get a burner and be able to customize my own. Maybe a couple of years of CompSci is enough background to start learning this crap? I don't know where I'd start. Any ideas?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Here is some good basic stuff: <A HREF="http://www.thirdgen.org/tech/promintro.shtml" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thirdgen.org/tech/promintro.shtml</A> <P>Some decent info on hacking bins here: <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ecmguy.geo/tuning/do_tune.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/ecmguy.geo/tuning/do_tune.html</A> <P>Lots of info and subscriptions to a pretty good mailing list here: <A HREF="http://diy-efi.org/gmecm/" TARGET=_blank>http://diy-efi.org/gmecm/</A> <P>A board on prom stuff here:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.thirdgen.org/cgi-bin/messgboard/forumdisplay.cgi?action=topics&forum=DIY+PROM&number=10&DaysPrune=20&LastLogin=" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thirdgen.org/cgi-bin/messgboard/forumdisplay.cgi?action=topics&forum=DIY+PROM&number=10&DaysPrune=20&LastLogin=</A> <P>If you are willing to learn, and have lots of patience, it shouldn't take too long.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR> ||||reg||1
